00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.280
If you have a cat, you have probably witnessed&nbsp;
them chasing down the insects and spiders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.000
other crawly creatures that live in your&nbsp;
home. Our master of microscopes, James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.440
has two cats. And so he is the beneficiary&nbsp;
of their combined hunting prowess.&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.840
So James decided to turn that&nbsp;
bounty over to the microscope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.640
and to see how these familiar creatures&nbsp;
look when we see them up close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.760
and also to see what autofluorescence they&nbsp;
reveal when we use our fluorescent light.&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.120
Now, this is a bit different than our usual path&nbsp;
into more microbial realms, where little droplets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.960
of water reveal new worlds and new creatures.&nbsp;
Instead this time, we’re looking at creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.200
who are quite visible and even familiar.
But it’s always fun to see familiar sights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.840
in unfamiliar ways, and to see what&nbsp;
else there might be there to learn.&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.800
For example, let’s talk about the silverfish.&nbsp;
Now, if you don’t recognize that name, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.160
the silverfish sounds like an elegant creature&nbsp;
you’d find swimming in a fantastical river.&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.040
But in reality they are those tiny, wingless&nbsp;
insects that you find scurrying around in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.560
humid spots like your bathroom. And our&nbsp;
homes are full of their favorite foods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.000
whether that’s the glue binding a book together,&nbsp;
wallpaper, or dry food in our pantries.&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.920
And unfortunately we don’t have footage of the&nbsp;
next thing we’re about to tell you, but it’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.840
kind of thing that when you learn it, you have&nbsp;
to share it.. The silverfish you are looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.640
right now might be remarkably still, but that&nbsp;
stillness... conceals the heart of a dancer.&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.760
When it comes time for reproduction, male and&nbsp;
female silverfish have a whole routine that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.960
work through. They begin by facing each other&nbsp;
and tapping their antenna against each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.760
before backing away. And then they repeat&nbsp;
this, coming closer and then coming apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:20.080
until the male silverfish runs away. Then the&nbsp;
female tracks him down, and when she does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:27.520
they lay head-to-tail—vibrating with reproductive&nbsp;
intent. The male will deposit a sperm capsule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.800
on the ground, the female will&nbsp;
pick it up. And from there a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:38.160
generation of crawling silverfish will be born.
Unfortunately, we do not have a fun story like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.320
that to tell about this spider for the very simple&nbsp;
reason that we don’t know what kind of spider it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.240
is. All we know about it is what you can see: it&nbsp;
is tiny and green with little bits of white hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.120
And its eyes! If you hate spiders, maybe&nbsp;
those eyes are scary, but we think they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.640
actually really lovely and a little endearing.
We also know that this was quite the lucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.840
spider because it was not killed by James’ cats,&nbsp;
which is why you can see it moving around…unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.920
this unfortunate spider, which met its end before&nbsp;
we put it under the microscope, but it seems to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.800
twitching a bit when we turn the UV light on.
Now we didn’t put our mystery green friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.160
under the UV light in case that&nbsp;
might’ve fried it to death.&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.200
Instead James decided to release it, so hopefully&nbsp;
it is now off living a wonderful life somewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.000
But we would love to know&nbsp;
what kind of spider it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.840
so if any of you know anything that can help us&nbsp;
identify it, please let us know in the comments!&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.680
Now, this, however, is part of a very&nbsp;
familiar insect. These are the little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.400
fuzzy hairs on a housefly’s butt.
And here, up close, are its wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.680
with hints of iridescence popping against the&nbsp;
dark background. That color is not the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.200
of pigments. It’s a structure inside the wing&nbsp;
itself that bounce light back in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.520
produces the multifaceted, filmy color we see.
And when we turn the fluorescent light on, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.040
iridescence goes away, but the patterns of veins&nbsp;
running through the wings becomes more apparent.&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.040
If we travel further down, we get to one&nbsp;
of the fly’s feet, which has some strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.440
autofluorescence at the side. Now, we’re&nbsp;
not entomologists, but our guess is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.520
the autofluorescence is connected to structures&nbsp;
in the feet that help the fly taste their food.&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:27.360
That’s right. Flies taste with their feet. As&nbsp;
they’re flying around and landing on potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.240
food, they use the taste receptors on their&nbsp;
feet to get a sense of whether their landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.560
spot is actually edible. Unfortunately, of course&nbsp;
those edible landing spots happen to be my food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.560
or yours, but mostly I’m concerned about mine.
Now, flies however are a much less threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.960
household pest compared to this one: the scorpion.
Now just to reassure you: there was no cat versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.360
scorpion showdown to bring you this image.&nbsp;
This scorpion was a souvenir, preserved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.960
bought in Thailand, and then brought to James’&nbsp;
home by the previous tenants of that home.&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.920
And when we turn on the UV light, almost&nbsp;
the entire scorpion seems to turn blue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.840
It’s a handy trick for finding scorpions&nbsp;
in the dark: just shine a black light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.800
and you’ll find them glowing around you.
But it’s not clear exactly what chemical causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.000
this neon shine, just that whatever chemical&nbsp;
it is, it’s located in an outer layer of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.760
scorpion’s exoskeleton. That is why scorpions that&nbsp;
have molted and are still growing out their new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.360
tough exoskeleton will not fluoresce.
Now, why does the scorpion do this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.680
We don’t really know, but one possibility is that&nbsp;
it turns the scorpion into a giant UV sensor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.920
able to detect UV light even when it's nighttime&nbsp;
so that it can find darker places to hide in.&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.960
And from one stinger to another, let’s&nbsp;
end with every picnic’s favorite insect:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.640
the bee. Only the familiar yellow and black colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.400
have been rendered into a more&nbsp;
orange and teal combination here.&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.360
This image of its eyes seems even more abstract,&nbsp;
but maybe that’s fitting because bee eyes…well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.960
they see things differently than we do.&nbsp;
They can’t see red, but they can see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.840
the ultraviolet spectrum. And so just as the way&nbsp;
that we look through the microscope changes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.960
world that we see, an animal’s eyes change the&nbsp;
world it sees. There are flowers with distinctive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.960
patterns painted in ultraviolet, a world that&nbsp;
will always remain abstract and invisible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:42.160
us. It’s a world layered on our own, imagined with&nbsp;
a vocabulary that can never fully do it justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.000
But not all visions are meant to be seen&nbsp;
by us. Sometimes it’s enough to just know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.280
that they exist, and to bring what little&nbsp;
parts of them we can into our own world.&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.840
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

